window.SV_INDEX=[{"p":"Charter","f":"charter.html","id":"charter-document","h":"The charter document","t":"Topic 16 The charter document Every field, from sponsor to sign-off. Project charter Sentinel Valley 2029: One Health Pandemic Early-Warning and Response Network Signed at M1 · Feb 26, 2027 Project title Sentinel Valley 2029: One Health Pandemic Early-Warning and Response Network Sponsor Chacha & the University of the Fraser Valley (recipient of the $1,000,000,000 investment) Champion UFV President and Vice-Chancellor ( see below ) Project manager Anmol, Sentinel Valley Project Management Office (PMO), UFV Abbotsford Start / go-live / close January 4, 2027 / October 1, 2029 / December 31, 2029 Budget $1,000,000,000, including a $100M contingency reserve and a $68.6M operating reserve for 2030–2034 ( Cost page ) Problem The Fraser Valley is a likely place for animal-to-human spillover, depends on outside supply routes that floods have cut, and shut down in 2020 because it was not prepared for a pandemic. Objectives (SMART) Operate 25 wastewater sentinel sites, 400 farm sentinel sites and 40 primary-care sentinel clinics, reporting signals to public-health officials within 48 hours, by June 30, 2029. Deliver a validated Fraser Valley Digital Twin that can simulate an outbreak and 5 response options within 24 hours, by June 29, 2029. Upgrade air in 103 public schools, 45 care facilities, 160 farm-worker and processing sites, 4 UFV campuses and 30 community hubs to at least 5 equivalent air changes per hour, by August 30, 2029. Open a flood-proof regional depot stocked with 10 million PPE items and a 100-bed surge unit, by July 31, 2029. Train 900 UFV students and 2,000 volunteers, and complete 1,100 continuity plans, by December 14, 2029. Success criteria All five objectives accepted through scope validation. Spending within budget (CPI ≥ 0.95). The full-scale exercise \"Operation Sentinel\" (Sept 2029) turns a simulated signal into an official alert within 72 hours. Approach A program of five coordinated projects. Construction follows a Waterfall life cycle; the digital twin and app follow a Spiral life cycle. Phase exits and kill points sit at each milestone. Key assumptions Partners (Fraser Health, BCCDC, CFIA, municipalities, school districts) sign agreements by April 2027. No health data is used before privacy impact assessments are approved. Constraints Fixed budget. A hard close date of Dec 31, 2029. School work only in summer. UFV has no legal authority over health orders. Roles Pillar leads: SENSE (UFV Agriculture Centre of Excellence), SIMULATE (UFV Computing Information Systems), SHIELD (facilities engineering lead), SUPPLY (logistics lead), SUSTAIN (UFV Health Sciences). A steering committee meets monthly. Comments Fraser Health: data access requires a signed information-sharing agreement. Finance: the contingency can only be released by the steering committee. Sign-off Sponsor, champion, project manager and Fraser Health liaison (signatures required at M1, Feb 26, 2027) Sponsor Champion Project manager Fraser Health liaison"},{"p":"Charter","f":"charter.html","id":"champion","h":"Who is the champion?","t":"Topic 7 Who is the champion? The person who selects, protects and opens doors for the project. A champion selects and protects a project, gets resources and approvals on time, and wins cooperation from other departments (Renso, 2026). My champion is the President and Vice-Chancellor of UFV. The President can work with the university, the cities of Abbotsford and Chilliwack, and Fraser Health. The President is also the public face of UFV in the region. The champion has four specific jobs, and is backed by a second, technical champion who gives the project credibility with farmers. Executive champion UFV President and Vice-Chancellor Chairs the steering committee Unblocks partner agreements in the first 120 days Defends the contingency reserve from early spending Publicly launches the network at go-live Technical champion Director, UFV Agriculture Centre of Excellence A second, technical champion, the director of the UFV Agriculture Centre of Excellence in Chilliwack, gives credibility with farmers. In 2014, the centre had a barn with poultry and livestock areas (University of the Fraser Valley, 2014)."},{"p":"Charter","f":"charter.html","id":"","h":"Project charter","t":"The charter formally authorizes the project and names who is responsible for it (Renso, 2026). In my last assignment I lost a mark on the charter, so this one includes every field.","top":1},{"p":"Closing","f":"closing.html","id":"the-closing-process","h":"The closing process","t":"Topic 25 The closing process Six steps between go-live and the formal close. Final scope validation Oct 2029 Each of the 12 deliverables is signed off by its acceptor using the checklist written in planning. Close procurements Oct–Nov 2029 Confirm vendors delivered, release holdbacks, settle claims, archive contracts. Handover to operations Nov 2029 The network moves from the project team to a permanent \"Sentinel Valley Operations Unit\" at UFV, with staff, manuals and the $68.6M operating reserve for 2030–2034. Lessons learned Dec 2029 Answer the four questions from class. Did we meet scope, time and cost? What went right? What went wrong? What will we do differently next time? (Renso, 2026) Release resources and celebrate Dec 2029 Team members get performance appraisals and references, and the project is celebrated with the community. Archive and final report By Dec 21, 2029 All documents are archived, and the final report goes to the sponsor. The project is formally closed on December 31, 2029 . Lessons learned The four questions from class Did we meet scope, time and cost? What went right? What went wrong? What will we do differently next time?"},{"p":"Closing","f":"closing.html","id":"after-closing","h":"After closing","t":"After closing How the network stays alive after the ribbon-cutting. Many preparedness projects fail after the ribbon-cutting. Equipment sits unused and skills fade. The federal mobile hospitals are an example: they cost about $7 million in one year just to store (Canadian Press, 2025). Sentinel Valley avoids this in three ways Used every flu season The surge unit and stockpile are used every flu season. A weekly forecast The digital twin produces a weekly respiratory forecast, so people use it in normal times. Skills stay local UFV keeps training graduates, so the skills stay in the valley. Because the network is used every week, it will be working when an outbreak happens."},{"p":"Closing","f":"closing.html","id":"","h":"Closing the project by December 31, 2029","t":"The project continues for three months after go-live. Closing formalizes acceptance of the project (Renso, 2026).","top":1},{"p":"Cost","f":"cost.html","id":"the-1b-at-a-glance","h":"The $1B at a glance","t":"The $1B at a glance Share of the budget by pillar. Hover a segment for its total. SENSE 14.9% SIMULATE 11.2% SHIELD 23.8% SUPPLY 20.0% SUSTAIN 8.3% PM 5% RESERVES 16.9% $148.8M SENSE $111.8M SIMULATE $237.7M SHIELD $199.9M SUPPLY $83.3M SUSTAIN $50.0M PM & quality $168.6M Reserves"},{"p":"Cost","f":"cost.html","id":"budget-map","h":"Budget map","t":"Budget map Click any block to see its quantity × unit cost calculation. Select any block to see its quantity × unit cost calculation. Budget map. Block size is proportional to cost. Colours: SENSE green, SIMULATE blue, SHIELD teal, SUPPLY ochre, SUSTAIN purple, management grey, reserves light grey."},{"p":"Cost","f":"cost.html","id":"cost-management","h":"Cost management","t":"Topic 3 Cost management Planning, estimating, budgeting and controlling. Cost management covers planning, estimating, budgeting and controlling (Renso, 2026). This proposal is a rough order of magnitude (ROM) estimate. It answers \"what will this cost?\" before the design exists. Estimates at this stage can be off by −30% to +50% (Alberta Infrastructure, 2025), which is why I hold a 10% contingency. The definitive estimate comes after detailed design (milestone M2), using RFQ prices from real vendors. Direct costs include the lab, HVAC equipment and PPE. Indirect costs include the PMO, electricity, and insurance inside the building lines. Intangible benefits, such as trust and UFV's reputation (Renso, 2026), are not given a dollar value. Construction-type items use benchmarks that already exist: B.C. and Ottawa spent $4.2 million on ventilation in 8 schools (Infrastructure Canada, 2024), about $525,000 per school, and that is my unit cost. Benchmark unit cost $525,000 per school ventilation upgrade: $4.2M for 8 B.C. schools (Infrastructure Canada, 2024) Learning from Ottawa's mistakes I also learned from Ottawa's mistakes. It paid more than $200 million for four mobile hospitals that mostly sat in storage (Canadian Press, 2025), and it discarded expired PPE in 2018–2019 (Health Canada, 2021). Our surge unit will be used every flu season, and stock will rotate through Fraser Health before it expires. We also set aside 6.9% of the budget to keep things running after 2029."},{"p":"Cost","f":"cost.html","id":"full-budget-51-lines","h":"Full budget (51 lines)","t":"Full budget (51 lines) Scroll inside the table; the header stays in place. Italic Assumption marks an estimate; everything else is cited. ID Item Quantity Unit cost Total Basis SENSE $148,832,600 1.1 Wastewater sentinel sites (autosamplers, cold chain, install) 25 sites $60,000 $1,500,000 Assumption – 25 sites: municipal plants incl. JAMES (Abbotsford/Mission/Sumas), hospitals, care homes, processing plants, UFV, YXX airport. Extends B.C.'s existing wastewater testing network (BC Centre for Disease Control [BCCDC], n.d.) 1.2 Wastewater multi-pathogen testing, 2028–2029 7,800 samples $517 $4,032,600 (Mathematica, n.d.) – 25 sites × 3 samples/week × 104 weeks; priced at the HIGH end of the reported US$34–$517 per-sample range 1.3 Barn-air & wild-bird sentinel sampling on poultry farms 400 farms $40,000 $16,000,000 Assumption – Air samplers + 2 years of sampling, with CFIA / BC Ministry of Agriculture partners. Today's poultry surveillance relies on passive reporting, targeted testing after detection, and pre-slaughter and hatchery sampling (Canadian Food Inspection Agency, 2022); routine barn sampling adds an earlier, active layer 1.4 Farm biosecurity upgrade grants (anterooms, wild-bird netting, filtered intakes) 450 farms $110,000 $49,500,000 Ontario's poultry biosecurity program pays up to $100,000 per location at 70% cost-share (Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada, 2022); B.C.'s pilot gives up to $30,000 per farm (B.C. Ministry of Agriculture and Food, 2025). Ours covers a full upgrade with no farmer share 1.5 UFV Chilliwack One Health genomics lab (BSL-2), building 18,000 sq ft $1,100 $19,800,000 Altus Vancouver range for university laboratories: $925–$1,400 per sq ft, hard costs (Altus Group, 2025) 1.6 Genomic sequencers, PCR & lab equipment 1 package $9,000,000 $9,000,000 Assumption – Benchtop sequencers, PCR machines and biosafety cabinets. Benchmark: Canada's CanCOGeN network received $40 million in federal funding to sequence up to 10,000 patients and 150,000 viral samples (Genome Canada, 2020); ours equips one regional lab 1.7 Surveillance & lab staff, 2028–2029 40 FTE-years $125,000 $5,000,000 (Job Bank, 2025) – 20 staff × 2 yrs; wage benchmark BC data scientist median $48.08/hr ≈ $100k + 25% benefits 1.8 Global Sentinel Partnership (spillover hotspots outside Canada) 1 grant $20,000,000 $20,000,000 (Renso, 2026) – Allowed: the $1B does not have to be spent in the Fraser Valley or Canada if it makes sense 1.9 Border & airport sampling (Sumas–Huntingdon crossing, YXX) 1 program $4,000,000 $4,000,000 Assumption – Population-level sampling only; no individual diagnosis. Precedent: PHAC ran airport wastewater pilots to assess COVID-19 variants coming into Canada (Public Health Agency of Canada, 2026) 1.10 Primary-care sentinel clinics (respiratory testing), 2028–2029 40 clinics $500,000 $20,000,000 Assumption – Human signal to pair with animal and wastewater signals. Expands B.C.'s Sentinel Practitioner Surveillance Network, where primary care providers contribute specimens for respiratory testing (BCCDC, 2026) SIMULATE $111,750,000 2.1 UFV Sentinel Data & Simulation Centre (building) 30,000 sq ft $1,000 $30,000,000 Altus Vancouver range for university/college buildings: $750–$1,250 per sq ft (Altus Group, 2025) 2.2 High-performance compute (GPU) + secure Canadian cloud, 3 yrs 1 package $22,000,000 $22,000,000 Benchmark: the national research computing alliance is investing up to $40M in one year for dedicated AI compute (Digital Research Alliance of Canada, n.d.); ours is a regional system over 3 years, running the agent-based digital twin (Bilal et al., 2025) 2.3 Digital-twin & AI development team, 2027–2029 150 FTE-years $125,000 $18,750,000 (Job Bank, 2025) – ~45 developers/data scientists/ modellers × ~2.9 yrs 2.4 Data-sharing agreements, privacy impact assessments, legal 1 package $6,000,000 $6,000,000 Assumption – Required before any health data is touched: B.C.'s privacy law sets out privacy impact assessment duties for public bodies (Freedom of Information and Protection of Privacy Act, 1996, s. 69) 2.5 Cybersecurity program + ISO/IEC 27001 certification 1 package $7,000,000 $7,000,000 (Renso, 2026) – Professor emphasises ISO certification 2.6 Public dashboard + multilingual alert app (English, Punjabi and Spanish) 1 product $9,000,000 $9,000,000 Assumption – Pull + push communication channel, as the communication plan requires (Renso, 2026) 2.7 UFV student research & co-op fund (CIS, data, health) 1 fund $11,000,000 $11,000,000 Assumption – Builds local talent; about 360 co-op terms. Federal Student Work Placement subsidies cover only up to $5,000–$7,000 per placement (Employment and Social Development Canada, 2019), so this fund pays the full term 2.8 Integration with BCCDC / Fraser Health systems (secure APIs) 1 package $8,000,000 $8,000,000 Assumption – Benchmark: Ottawa committed $505 million over five years to CIHI, Canada Health Infoway and federal data partners (Canadian Healthcare Techn"},{"p":"Cost","f":"cost.html","id":"controlling-cost-with-earned-value","h":"Controlling cost with earned value","t":"Controlling cost with earned value Earned value compares spending with the work completed (Renso, 2026). The PMO reports earned value monthly. Example for the end of Q2 2028: $420M Planned value (PV) $395M Earned value (EV) $410M Actual cost (AC) Cost variance −$15M CV = EV − AC = −$15M (over budget for the work done) Schedule variance −$25M SV = EV − PV = −$25M (behind schedule) Cost performance index 0.96 CPI = EV ÷ AC = 0.96 Under my rule, a CPI below 0.95 or an SV worse than −5% of PV triggers a recovery plan. In this example the CPI is still acceptable, but the schedule needs attention."},{"p":"Cost","f":"cost.html","id":"roi","h":"Return on investment and payback","t":"Topic 14 Return on investment and payback Three scenarios, 2027–2049, discounted at 3%. ROI compares total discounted benefits with total discounted costs, and payback is the time it takes to recover the investment (Renso, 2026). My model and its assumptions are below. B.C.'s real GDP in 2019 was about $278 billion (derived from BC Stats, 2024). In 2020 it fell 3.1% instead of growing about 2.9%, a gap of roughly 6%. The Fraser Valley is about 6.2% of B.C.'s population (Statistics Canada, 2022; Pacific Economic Development Canada, 2022). If the valley's share of the loss matched its share of the population, one pandemic year cost the valley an estimated $1.04 billion. How the $1.04B loss is estimated $278B B.C. real GDP in 2019 (derived from BC Stats, 2024) × ~6% gap fell 3.1% instead of growing about 2.9% × 6.2% the Fraser Valley's share of B.C.'s population ≈ $1.04B estimated loss to the valley in one pandemic year Low scenario −78% No pandemic before 2050; $15M/yr from fewer sick days and fewer avian-flu losses Benefits $300M Simple ROI -70% Discounted ROI -78% Payback Not reached Base scenario −29% One pandemic in 2035; losses cut by 40%; $25M/yr Benefits $915M Simple ROI -8.5% Discounted ROI -29% Payback Not reached by 2049 High scenario +2.5% One pandemic in 2035; losses cut by 60%; $35M/yr Benefits $1,323M Simple ROI +32% Discounted ROI +2.5% Payback 2040 In conclusion, Sentinel Valley pays for itself only if a serious pandemic happens and the network works well. The model leaves out lives saved and avoided government emergency spending, because these are hard to measure. For scale, CERB alone was projected at $71.3 billion (CBC News, 2020b). This is preparedness spending, like insurance. Its value shows up in the years when something goes wrong, which is why the business case is based on protecting the region from losses."},{"p":"Cost","f":"cost.html","id":"","h":"Cost: where every dollar goes","t":"The professor asked for every dollar to be explained, so all $1,000,000,000 is assigned below. Researched prices are cited, and estimates are marked as assumptions.","top":1},{"p":"Home","f":"index.html","id":"what-would-i-build-in-december-2019","h":"What would I build in December 2019?","t":"The time-machine question What would I build in December 2019? When I started this proposal, I asked myself a \"time machine\" question: if I could go back to December 2019 with what we know and the technology we have today, what would I build in the Fraser Valley? One hospital or one app would not have been enough. COVID-19 showed that several things fail together. Warnings arrive late, people spread the virus before they feel sick, indoor air carries it, supplies run out and, in the end, whole communities shut down (Independent Panel for Pandemic Preparedness and Response, 2021). What failed together in 2020 Warnings arrive late People spread the virus before they feel sick Indoor air carries it Supplies run out Whole communities shut down"},{"p":"Home","f":"index.html","id":"the-valley-is-a-likely-spillover-point","h":"The valley is a likely spillover point","t":"Why here The valley is a likely spillover point The Fraser Valley also has a risk that most of Canada does not. About 80% of British Columbia's poultry farms are here (CTV News, 2022), and avian influenza has hit them in waves since 2022, with about 8.7 million birds culled in B.C. (CTV News, 2025). In November 2024, Canada's first locally acquired human case of H5N1 was a B.C. teenager. Fraser Health led the investigation, and the virus most closely matched wild birds found in the Fraser Valley (B.C. Ministry of Health, 2024a, 2024b). The next pandemic is a \"Disease X\" that nobody can name yet (World Health Organization [WHO], 2024b). But if it jumps from animals to people in Canada, our valley is one of the most likely places for that to happen. ~80% of B.C.'s poultry farms are in the Fraser Valley (CTV News, 2022) 8.7M birds culled in B.C. since 2022 (CTV News, 2025) Nov 2024 Canada's first locally acquired human H5N1 case (B.C. Ministry of Health, 2024a) Disease X The next pandemic is one nobody can name yet (WHO, 2024b)"},{"p":"Home","f":"index.html","id":"five-connected-capabilities-the-5s","h":"Five connected capabilities: the 5S","t":"Five connected capabilities: the 5S Because the valley is a likely spillover point, Sentinel Valley would make it an early-warning site for Canada, with five connected capabilities that I call the 5S: SENSE Surveillance $148.8M SIMULATE Simulation $111.8M SHIELD Clean air $237.7M SUPPLY Supplies $199.9M SUSTAIN Training $83.3M Bars compare each pillar's budget with the largest, SHIELD. See how they work together on the Day 0 page."},{"p":"Home","f":"index.html","id":"four-chapters","h":"Four chapters","t":"Explore the proposal Four chapters 01 The case Why the valley Day 0 simulation 02 The plan Charter Scope Time Cost 03 The people Organization People Stakeholders 04 Control & close Quality Risk & procurement Closing"},{"p":"Home","f":"index.html","id":"how-to-read-this-proposal","h":"How to read this proposal","t":"How to read this proposal Every one of the 25 course topics, linked to its section. Professor Renso asked for a visual charter that covers 25 course topics. Each page below lists the topics it covers, and this index links every topic to its section. The Time , Scope and Cost pages are separate, as the assignment asks. The Day 0 simulation gives a quick overview. It replays a 2030 outbreak twice: once without Sentinel Valley, and once with it. Time Scope Cost Project stakeholders Three spheres of system management Four frames of organizations (UFV) Champion Deliverables Life cycle model (Waterfall / Spiral) Initiating, planning, executing, monitoring & controlling, closing Pre-initiation tasks Progress reports SWOT analysis ROI / payback analysis Project quality management Project charter Testing & cost of quality Project HR management Motivating people Resource planning Communicating to stakeholders Top 10 risks & controlling risks Procurement management Handling stakeholders Closing process"},{"p":"Home","f":"index.html","id":"","h":"Sentinel Valley 2029 is an early-warning network for the next pandemic.","t":"A $1 billion proposal for Chacha & the University of the Fraser Valley: Canada's first One Health early-warning and response network, designed to help the Fraser Valley avoid another shutdown like 2020.","top":1},{"p":"Organization","f":"organization.html","id":"spheres","h":"Three spheres of system management","t":"Topic 5 Three spheres of system management Business, organization and technology have to work at the same time. Business view A $1B network for about 324,000 people makes sense if it protects a regional economy whose one-year pandemic loss I estimate at about $1 billion. It also protects the poultry industry, and the network keeps producing value between pandemics through flu-season data and training. Organization view Fraser Health must accept data from a university, farmers must allow sampling on their land, and school districts must schedule summer construction. The technology depends on these agreements. Technology view Sensors, lab equipment, a secure data platform, a digital twin and an alert app all have to connect. Most of the technology, like wastewater testing and HVAC, is already proven. Only the digital twin is new, and it is built in spiral cycles. Systems thinking means looking at a project as a whole, inside the organization where it has to work, not as a set of separate parts (Renso, 2026). A network that works technically can still fail if the business case is weak or the partners do not support it. That is why every pillar has an owner in each sphere: a business lead who tracks value for money, an organization lead who manages partner agreements, and a technical lead who makes the systems connect. When a change request arrives, I ask three questions Does it still make business sense? Will the partners accept it? Will the technology still fit together?"},{"p":"Organization","f":"organization.html","id":"frames","h":"Four frames of organizations: UFV","t":"Topic 6 Four frames of organizations: UFV Bolman and Deal (2017) describe four frames for understanding any organization. The lectures ask us to apply them to UFV (Renso, 2026). Structural frame UFV is organized into faculties, campuses and administrative units. Sentinel Valley needs people from Agriculture, Computing Information Systems, Health Sciences and Facilities to work together. I propose a matrix structure: staff stay in their faculty but report to a pillar lead for project work. The PMO sits under the Provost, and the steering committee holds the decision rights. Human resource frame If the project simply adds work, people will resist. Because faculty and staff already have full workloads, the budget pays for course releases, new hires and co-op students (see People ), and gives faculty research opportunities they value. Political frame Resources are scarce, so campuses may compete for the labs and municipalities will want credit. Abbotsford gets the data centre and Chilliwack gets the genomics lab, close to the farms. Farm groups may fear that surveillance leads to culls. The champion's job is to negotiate these interests openly, and stakeholder agreements make the trade-offs explicit. Symbolic frame UFV's identity is a university that serves the Fraser Valley and works on Stó:lō territory. The name \"Sentinel Valley\" presents the valley as Canada's early-warning site, and launch events and the dashboard use that message."},{"p":"Organization","f":"organization.html","id":"lifecycle","h":"Project life cycle: Waterfall and Spiral","t":"Topic 9 Project life cycle: Waterfall and Spiral I use both models, because the deliverables are different. Buildings, HVAC and the depot Waterfall Buildings, HVAC and the depot follow Waterfall , because construction cannot be done in cycles. That work moves through analysis, design, construction, testing and support in order (Renso, 2026). Digital twin, alert app, dashboard Spiral The digital twin, alert app and dashboard follow Spiral . The lecture describes Spiral as non-linear, revisiting all phases. Each cycle plans, builds, tests and evaluates a version with real public-health users, then the next cycle improves it. Four cycles run from January 2028 to June 2029. The whole project also moves through the concept, development, implementation and close-out phases, with phase exits and kill points at each milestone (Renso, 2026). Running two models side by side needs one rule: the spiral software must be ready to plug into the buildings when they open. Each spiral cycle ends with a demonstration to public-health users, and cycle 4 must finish before integrated testing starts in June 2029. If a cycle runs late, I cut features from the next cycle instead of moving the deadline, which protects the critical path on the Time page ."},{"p":"Organization","f":"organization.html","id":"process","h":"The five process groups in this project","t":"Topic 10 The five process groups in this project What happens in each group, and when. Initiating Jan–Apr 2027 Charter signed, champion and sponsor confirmed, stakeholder register, partner MOUs Planning Mar–Sep 2027 Requirements, WBS, schedule, budget, privacy assessments, risk, quality, communication and procurement plans Executing Jun 2027–Sep 2029 Building the five pillars, hiring, training, contracting Monitoring & controlling Jan 2027–Dec 2029 Monthly progress reports, earned value, risk register reviews, change control. This runs during all the other groups Closing Oct–Dec 2029 Acceptance, contract close-out, lessons learned, handover to operations The process groups overlap; they do not happen one after another. Executing means coordinating people and resources to carry out the plans, while monitoring and controlling means measuring progress and taking corrective action (Renso, 2026). In Sentinel Valley, planning continues during execution because each spiral cycle of the digital twin is planned just before it starts. If the earned-value numbers on the Cost page slip, the project goes back to planning and adjusts the baseline through change control. Each group also ends with a clear output: a signed charter, an approved baseline, accepted deliverables and a final report."},{"p":"Organization","f":"organization.html","id":"preinit","h":"Pre-initiation tasks","t":"Topic 11 Pre-initiation tasks Six tasks come before the project officially starts (Renso, 2026). Each task is covered as follows: Determine scope, time and cost Done at ROM level in this proposal. Identify the sponsor Chacha & UFV. Select a project manager I lead the PMO, supported by an experienced, PMP-certified deputy. Develop a business case The Why page and the ROI analysis. Hold meetings to agree on process and expectations A kickoff with Fraser Health, BCCDC, CFIA and the school districts in December 2026. Determine size and split into mini-projects The 5S pillars are five manageable projects inside one program."},{"p":"Organization","f":"organization.html","id":"","h":"Organization","t":"Every project depends on the organization that carries it (Renso, 2026). Here I analyze Sentinel Valley as a system, and UFV as the organization that has to carry it.","top":1},{"p":"People","f":"people.html","id":"hr","h":"Project human resource management","t":"Topic 18 Project human resource management HR planning started with the four processes from the lectures: plan, acquire, develop and manage the team (Renso, 2026). Plan Acquire Develop Manage the team Peak headcount by role, and whether we hire or partner PMO (PM, schedulers, cost controllers, procurement officers) Hire; deputy PM is PMP-certified 18 Data scientists, software developers, modellers (digital twin) Hire + UFV CIS co-op students 45 Cybersecurity and privacy officers Hire 6 Lab technologists and genomic analysts Hire; partner with BCCDC for training 20 Field samplers (wastewater, farms) Hire locally + farm partners 25 HVAC engineers and contractors Procured through RFP ~120 Logistics and depot staff Hire 15 Instructors, community and Stó:lō liaisons UFV faculty + liaisons hired with Stó:lō guidance 30 The wage benchmark for technical roles is the B.C. median for data scientists, $48.08 per hour (Job Bank, 2025), or about $100,000 a year. I add 25% for benefits, giving $125,000 per year in the budget. A shortage of qualified people is a top reason organizations outsource today (Renso, 2026). Where B.C. lacks skills, we bring in contractors in the short term while UFV trains locals for the long term. From wage to budget line $48.08 / hour ≈ $100,000 / year + 25% benefits $125,000 / FTE-year"},{"p":"People","f":"people.html","id":"motivate","h":"How I will motivate people","t":"Topic 19 How I will motivate people Intrinsic motivation comes from enjoying the work, and extrinsic motivation comes from rewards or penalties (Renso, 2026). This project has a rare intrinsic advantage: people get to protect their own families and neighbours. I will use that, along with the methods below. Maslow Following Maslow, salaries and job security cover the basic needs, and the team meets the people it protects. Building a new regional digital twin gives people the kind of creative work Maslow places at the top (Renso, 2026). Herzberg Herzberg calls salary, benefits and good equipment hygiene factors: they prevent dissatisfaction but do not motivate. The real motivators are achievement, recognition, responsibility and growth (Renso, 2026). Milestones will be celebrated publicly, and pillar leads will have real authority. Each staff member also gets a training budget. Influence & power I want to lead mainly through expert and referent power: knowledge and trust. Thamhain and Wilemon found \"work challenge\" and \"expertise\" to be strong influence bases (Renso, 2026). Five dysfunctions The five team dysfunctions are absence of trust, fear of conflict, lack of commitment, avoidance of accountability and inattention to results (Renso, 2026). I reduce them by keeping working teams at 3–7 people, holding short regular meetings, never blaming in public, and putting results on a shared dashboard."},{"p":"People","f":"people.html","id":"resources","h":"Resource planning","t":"Topic 20 Resource planning Assigning work, levelling peaks and booking scarce skills early. Finalize requirements Decide how work will be done Break down the work Assign it through an OBS Work is assigned through the steps from the lectures: finalize requirements, decide how work will be done, break down the work, then assign it through an organizational breakdown structure (Renso, 2026). The biggest danger is resource overloading. Developers are needed heavily in 2028 and only for tweaks later, while HVAC crews are needed only in summer. I level resources: temporary contractors cover the 2028 development peak, and HVAC crews work on care homes and farm housing between the two school summers. To assign work, I use a responsibility assignment matrix, so every WBS item has exactly one person accountable for it. The real constraint is scarce skills: certified air-balancing technicians and genomic analysts are hard to hire in B.C. I book them early through procurement contracts and the BCCDC training partnership, and the PMO keeps a quarterly resource chart so that an overload shows up months before it happens, not on the day work is late. Developers peak in 2028 Temporary contractors cover the 2028 development peak. HVAC crews are seasonal Between the two school summers, crews work on care homes and farm housing. Scarce skills booked early Air-balancing technicians and genomic analysts, through procurement contracts and the BCCDC training partnership."},{"p":"People","f":"people.html","id":"","h":"Team","t":"A good team needs more than a good budget (Renso, 2026). Sentinel Valley needs about 300 people at its peak, many with skills the valley does not have yet.","top":1},{"p":"Quality","f":"quality.html","id":"qm","h":"Project quality management","t":"Topic 15 Project quality management Quality management has three processes (Renso, 2026). In Sentinel Valley: Process 1 Planning quality Each deliverable gets measurable standards. A school room passes at five or more equivalent air changes per hour (CDC, n.d.). A wastewater result reaches officials within 48 hours. The digital twin's forecasts are checked against real flu-season data. Process 2 Quality assurance Independent audits check that we follow our own processes. One example is checking that every data request had a privacy approval. Process 3 Quality control We inspect actual results. HVAC flow tests, lab proficiency tests and app penetration tests produce validated deliverables. I benchmark against existing systems, such as BCCDC wastewater surveillance (BCCDC, n.d.), and use Six Sigma's DMAIC cycle (define, measure, analyze, improve, control) to cut sample turnaround time. For security, the data platform will earn ISO/IEC 27001 certification. ISO certification gives partners outside proof that our processes meet an international standard, which helps when we ask Fraser Health to trust a university with health data. Six Sigma DMAIC: cutting sample turnaround time Define Measure Analyze Improve Control"},{"p":"Quality","f":"quality.html","id":"cause-and-effect-diagram-an-alert-reached-","h":"Cause-and-effect diagram: \"an alert reached officials late\"","t":"Cause-and-effect diagram: \"an alert reached officials late\" Each cause has a prevention measure, such as backup samplers, a weekend rota, a second sequencer and a road-independent courier plan. Possible causes, by category: People (sampler on leave, analyst not trained); Process (no weekend lab shift, unclear escalation); Technology (sequencer down, API to Fraser Health failed); Environment (a flood closed roads to the lab). Each cause has a prevention measure, such as backup samplers, a weekend rota, a second sequencer and a road-independent courier plan."},{"p":"Quality","f":"quality.html","id":"coq","h":"Testing and cost of quality","t":"Topic 17 Testing and cost of quality Testing finds problems, and quality management also prevents them (American Society for Quality [ASQ], n.d.). Our testing plan has four layers: 4 · Operation Sentinel (Sept 2029) A full-scale Disease X exercise with all partners. It must pass before go-live. 3 · User acceptance testing Fraser Health and school districts test with their real workflows. 2 · Integration testing (Jun–Aug 2029) Signals flow from site to lab to digital twin to dashboard to the official. 1 · Unit testing Each sensor, lab method and software module on its own. Testing builds from the bottom up: each layer must pass before the one above it. The cost of quality is the price of conformance plus the cost of nonconformance (Renso, 2026). The cards show which budget lines cover each category. Cost of conformance Prevention · lines 2.5, 3.8, 5.1 Training, ISO 27001 program, building assessments, design reviews Appraisal (incl. test equipment) · lines 3.7, 6.2, 5.3 Air-flow testing, lab proficiency tests, independent audits, Operation Sentinel Cost of nonconformance Internal failure · Contingency 7.1 Re-doing an HVAC install that fails testing before handover External failure · Avoided by spending on prevention and appraisal A missed outbreak signal, or a false alarm that closes schools unnecessarily: lost trust and possibly lives"},{"p":"Quality","f":"quality.html","id":"","h":"Quality","t":"Quality means the project satisfies the needs it was started for (Renso, 2026). For a warning system, a false alarm or a missed signal would both damage trust.","top":1},{"p":"References","f":"references.html","id":"ai-use","h":"Statement on AI use","t":"Statement on AI use How AI tools were used in this proposal. I used Claude (Anthropic) to help research sources, draft and edit the proposal text, check the budget arithmetic and build the website code. The project idea, the choice of Sentinel Valley, the research direction and the final review and edits are my own."},{"p":"References","f":"references.html","id":"","h":"References","t":"The evidence library. Every researched fact in this proposal is cited in APA 7 style; estimates are marked as assumptions on the Cost page. The $1 billion investment is a course scenario. Search, filter or browse every source below.","top":1},{"p":"Risk & procurement","f":"risk.html","id":"swot","h":"SWOT analysis","t":"Topic 13 SWOT analysis Internal strengths and weaknesses, external opportunities and threats. Helpful Harmful Internal Strengths (internal) UFV's Agriculture Centre of Excellence, with a barn that has a poultry area, located among the farms UFV computing and health programs to train local talent Builds on systems that already work, such as BCCDC wastewater testing Weaknesses (internal) UFV has never run a $1B program No legal authority over public-health orders The digital twin is new technology with uncertainty External Opportunities (external) WHO and CEPI urging preparedness for whole pathogen families (WHO, 2024a) Clean air also cuts ordinary flu and cold sick days every year The model could be exported to other farming regions in Canada Threats (external) A pandemic arriving before 2029 Another atmospheric-river flood during construction Public distrust of surveillance; tariffs raising equipment prices SWOT is useful only if it changes the plan (Renso, 2026). So each quadrant feeds a decision. The strengths (UFV's farm-based research centre and computing programs) are used to seize the biggest opportunity, which is getting ready for whole families of pathogens. The weaknesses become entries in the risk register below: inexperience with a $1B program is handled by a PMP-certified deputy and independent audits, and the lack of legal authority is handled by partner agreements. The threats, such as a flood or an early pandemic, are why the depot sits on high ground and why the surge unit and stockpile arrive before go-live."},{"p":"Risk & procurement","f":"risk.html","id":"risks","h":"Top 10 risks and how I control them","t":"Topic 22 Top 10 risks and how I control them Following \"top 10 risk item tracking\" (Renso, 2026), each risk has a root cause, an owner, a probability and an impact, both scored from 1 to 5. The score is probability × impact. The register is reviewed monthly, and the status column shows the trend. 5 4 1 3 6 4 8 2 5 3 10 9 7 2 1 1 2 3 4 5 Heat map of the top 10 risks. Horizontal: probability (1–5). Vertical: impact (1–5). Hover a number for the risk. Highest scores 2 Farmers resist sampling 16 5 Cost overrun 16 1 Partners refuse to share health data 15 3 Depot construction delay 15 6 Cyberattack on the data platform 15 Score = probability × impact. Full register with owners and responses below. # Risk Root cause Owner P I Score Response Status 1 Partners refuse to share health data Privacy fears, legal limits Champion 3 5 15 Mitigate: privacy impact assessments first, ISO 27001, population-level data only Open 2 Farmers resist sampling Fear of culls SENSE lead 4 4 16 Mitigate: biosecurity grants, Poultry Association co-design Open 3 Depot construction delay (critical path) Permits, weather, labour SUPPLY lead 3 5 15 Mitigate: PERT buffer, penalty clauses; transfer: fixed-price contract Open 4 Flood during construction Atmospheric rivers (CBC News, 2021) PM 2 5 10 Avoid: site on high ground; insurance Open 5 Cost overrun Early ROM estimate (−30% to +50%) Cost controller 4 4 16 Accept with $100M contingency; earned-value control Open 6 Cyberattack on the data platform A health-data target Security officer 3 5 15 Mitigate: ISO 27001, penetration tests, Canadian hosting Open 7 Shortage of skilled staff B.C. labour market HR lead 4 3 12 Mitigate: UFV co-op pipeline, contractors for peaks Open 8 Digital twin gives wrong forecasts New technology, poor data SIMULATE lead 3 4 12 Mitigate: spiral cycles, validation against real seasons, human review Open 9 Equipment price increases (tariffs) Trade tensions Procurement lead 3 3 9 Share: price-adjustment clauses; buy early Open 10 Stockpile expires unused The NESS mistake (Health Canada, 2021) SUPPLY lead 2 3 6 Avoid: rotation contracts with Fraser Health Open Positive risk Exploit and enhance Some risks are positive. One is federal co-funding to copy the model in other provinces. I would exploit it by documenting everything as a reusable playbook, and enhance it by inviting PHAC to the Operation Sentinel exercise (Renso, 2026). Every month Controlling risk Controlling risk means re-scoring the register every month, issuing change requests when a response changes the plan, and treating the contingency as a managed reserve."},{"p":"Risk & procurement","f":"risk.html","id":"procure","h":"Procurement management","t":"Topic 23 Procurement management UFV cannot build most of this itself. Plan what to buy and when Conduct procurements and award contracts Control vendor relationships Close contracts Procurement has four stages: plan what to buy and when, conduct procurements and award contracts, control vendor relationships, and close contracts (Renso, 2026). Where the solution is open, I use an RFP to ask vendors to propose how. Where the item is clearly specified, I use an RFQ to ask only for price. RFP The solution is open: vendors propose how . RFQ The item is clearly specified: vendors give only a price . What UFV needs to buy Method Contract type Award by Depot design-build RFP Fixed price + late penalties Nov 2027 Genomics lab construction and equipment RFP (building) + RFQ (sequencers, PCR) Fixed price Nov 2027 Digital twin and app development partner RFP Time and materials with a cap, paid per spiral cycle Nov 2027 GPU compute and Canadian cloud RFQ 3-year service agreement Nov 2027 HVAC and far-UVC installation (schools, care homes) RFP, split into 4 regional lots Unit price per building Feb 2028 PPE, test kits and medical supplies RFQ, Canadian suppliers preferred Supply + rotation agreement Apr 2029 Modular surge unit RFP Fixed price, with a maintenance clause Mar 2028 Mobile clinic vehicles RFQ Fixed price Dec 2028 I also learned from the federal mobile-hospital contracts. They were sole-sourced in a rush and ended up in storage (Canadian Press, 2025). Contracts will be competitively tendered, and each asset will have an assigned user."},{"p":"Risk & procurement","f":"risk.html","id":"","h":"SWOT, risks and procurement","t":"What could help, what could hurt, and how UFV buys what it cannot build itself.","top":1},{"p":"Scope","f":"scope.html","id":"scope-management-plan","h":"Scope management plan","t":"Topic 2 Scope management plan Six parts, from planning to control. Plan scope management Collect requirements Define scope Create the WBS Validate scope Control scope Scope work has six parts: plan scope management, collect requirements, define scope, create the WBS, validate scope and control scope (Renso, 2026). Requirements come from three workshops in the first 90 days with Fraser Health, farm associations and school districts. Every requirement gets an ID and an owner, and is traced to a deliverable. Skipping this step leads to expensive rework (Renso, 2026). In scope Surveillance of wastewater, farms, wild birds, primary-care clinics and border points (population-level only) The Fraser Valley Digital Twin, data centre, alert app and dashboard Air upgrades in the buildings listed in the charter A flood-proof depot, stockpile, rotation contracts, surge unit and mobile fleet Training, exercises, continuity plans, community liaisons, and the 2030–2034 operating reserve Out of scope Building a new hospital or vaccine factory (too slow and outside UFV's role) Issuing health orders, quarantines or border closures (see Charter ) Tracking people's phones or locations; all surveillance is population-level Air upgrades for private homes and private businesses (except continuity-plan grants)"},{"p":"Scope","f":"scope.html","id":"deliverables","h":"Deliverables","t":"Topic 8 Deliverables A deliverable is a product or service the project hands over, such as a report, a training session or a segment of code (Renso, 2026). Sentinel Valley has 12 major deliverables: D1 Management Signed charter and partner MOUs Sponsor Apr 30, 2027 D2 Management Baseline project plan (WBS, schedule, budget) Steering committee Sep 3, 2027 D3 SENSE 25-site wastewater network + border sampling BCCDC Jun 30, 2028 D4 SENSE One Health genomics lab (commissioned) UFV + CFIA Mar 30, 2029 D5 SENSE Farm, wild-bird and clinic sentinel program Fraser Health Jun 29, 2029 D6 SIMULATE Fraser Valley Digital Twin v4 + data centre Fraser Health MHO Jun 29, 2029 D7 SIMULATE Alert app + public dashboard (ISO 27001 certified) UFV IT security Jul 31, 2029 D8 SHIELD Air-upgraded buildings with test reports Building owners Aug 30, 2029 D9 SUPPLY Flood-proof depot, stockpile and rotation contracts Fraser Health Jul 31, 2029 D10 SUPPLY 100-bed surge unit + 10 mobile clinics Fraser Health Jun 29, 2029 D11 SUSTAIN Trained graduates, volunteers and continuity plans UFV Provost Dec 14, 2029 D12 Management Operation Sentinel exercise report + lessons learned Sponsor Dec 21, 2029 Each tile shows the deliverable, its pillar, who accepts it (bottom left) and its due date (bottom right)."},{"p":"Scope","f":"scope.html","id":"work-breakdown-structure","h":"Work breakdown structure","t":"Work breakdown structure The WBS breaks the work into levels (Renso, 2026). Level 1 is the project. Level 2 follows the process groups and the five pillars, and the same numbers appear on the Gantt chart: Sentinel Valley 2029 Initiating charter, stakeholders Planning requirements, privacy, design Procurement RFPs, RFQs, contracts SENSE wastewater, farms, lab, global partnership SIMULATE data centre, digital twin, app, ISO 27001 SHIELD assessments, schools, care homes, farm housing SUPPLY land, depot, stockpile, surge unit, fleet SUSTAIN training, continuity, exercises Testing & acceptance Monitoring & controlling Closing"},{"p":"Scope","f":"scope.html","id":"validating-and-controlling-scope","h":"Validating and controlling scope","t":"Keeping scope under control Validating and controlling scope Scope validation is the formal acceptance of finished deliverables (Renso, 2026). Each deliverable above has a named person who signs it off, using a checklist written during planning, not afterwards. Scope control goes through integrated change control. Any request is logged, its effect on time, cost, quality and risk is analyzed, and the steering committee approves or rejects it. Seventy percent of projects spend at least 10% of their time managing changes (Renso, 2026), so I have budgeted time for change requests. Integrated change control Log the request Analyze the effect on time, cost, quality and risk Steering committee approves or rejects 70% of projects spend at least 10% of their time managing changes (Renso, 2026)."},{"p":"Scope","f":"scope.html","id":"","h":"Scope","t":"Scope is the work that must be done (Renso, 2026). On a project this size, the biggest danger is scope creep: \"while we're at it, let's also build…\". This page lists what is in and out of scope.","top":1},{"p":"Day 0 simulation","f":"simulation.html","id":"the-flow-behind-the-scenario","h":"The flow behind the scenario","t":"The flow behind the scenario How the five pillars respond to a new signal. SENSE detects a signal and the lab confirms it within 24 hours, then SIMULATE tests response options in the digital twin. After public-health officials decide, SUPPLY and SHIELD provide testing, PPE and clean air, while SUSTAIN keeps people trained and informed. If no community spread shows up, normal life continues, and every exercise and real event updates the model. Flowchart: how the five pillars respond to a new signal. Only public-health officials can issue orders (amber diamond). Each step in the right-hand column uses a budget line that exists on the Cost page ."},{"p":"Day 0 simulation","f":"simulation.html","id":"","h":"Day 0: the same outbreak, two endings","t":"This is a fictional scenario that shows how the five pillars work together. A new avian flu variant spills over to a farm worker near Chilliwack in October 2030. Press play, or drag the slider to move through the days.","top":1},{"p":"Stakeholders","f":"stakeholders.html","id":"who","h":"Who the stakeholders are","t":"Topic 4 Who the stakeholders are The engagement levels come from the lectures: unaware, resistant, neutral, supportive and leading (Renso, 2026). The tracker shows where each group is now and where it needs to be. Stakeholder Unaware Resistant Neutral Supportive Leading Chacha & UFV leadership (sponsor) UFV faculty and staff Project team and PMO Fraser Health and medical health officers BCCDC and the Public Health Agency of Canada CFIA and the B.C. Ministry of Agriculture Poultry farmers and the B.C. Poultry Association Temporary foreign and local farm workers Stó:lō Nation communities School districts 33, 34, 75 and 78, parents Municipalities and the FVRD Vendors and contractors Residents and media Engagement now Target The bar shows how far each group has to move. Stakeholder Internal / external Main interest Engagement now Target Chacha & UFV leadership (sponsor) Internal Value for $1B, reputation Supportive Leading UFV faculty and staff Internal Workload, research opportunities Unaware Supportive Project team and PMO Internal Clear roles, recognition Neutral Leading Fraser Health and medical health officers External Data quality, legal authority, privacy Neutral Leading BCCDC and the Public Health Agency of Canada External Not duplicating national systems Neutral Supportive CFIA and the B.C. Ministry of Agriculture External Animal health, trade Neutral Supportive Poultry farmers and the B.C. Poultry Association External Fear that sampling means culls; biosecurity grants Resistant Supportive Temporary foreign and local farm workers External Safe housing, job security Unaware Supportive Stó:lō Nation communities External Respect, data sovereignty, equal benefit Unaware Leading partner School districts 33, 34, 75 and 78, parents External Safe classrooms, summer schedules Neutral Supportive Municipalities and the FVRD External Wastewater access, local jobs Neutral Supportive Vendors and contractors External Fair contracts, on-time payment Unaware Supportive Residents and media External Privacy, trust, clear information Unaware Supportive"},{"p":"Stakeholders","f":"stakeholders.html","id":"handling","h":"Handling stakeholders","t":"Topic 24 Handling stakeholders Stakeholder management has four processes: identify, plan, manage engagement and control engagement (Renso, 2026). Identify Plan Manage engagement Control engagement The hardest group Poultry farmers If they believe a positive sample means their whole flock is culled, they will refuse sampling. When avian flu is detected on a farm, CFIA requires all birds there to be euthanized. My plan gives farms biosecurity grants of about $110,000 each, which reduce the chance of infection in the first place, and it lets the B.C. Poultry Association help design the program. Leading partner Stó:lō communities Stó:lō communities decide how data about their members is used. Liaisons are hired with Stó:lō guidance and a community seat sits on the steering committee. Control engagement Re-assessed every quarter Engagement levels are re-assessed every quarter. If a key stakeholder slips back, for example from supportive to neutral, the champion meets them within two weeks."},{"p":"Stakeholders","f":"stakeholders.html","id":"comms","h":"Communicating to stakeholders","t":"Topic 21 Communicating to stakeholders The communication plan states who needs what information, when, in what format, from whom, and how issues are escalated (Renso, 2026). It uses all three methods from the lectures: interactive (two-way), push (sent out) and pull (people look it up). Interactive Two-way: meetings, briefings, stand-ups, working groups and town halls. Push Sent out: reports, emails, text messages and news releases. Pull People look it up: the website, the public dashboard and FAQs. Audience Information Method Frequency Sender Steering committee Progress report, earned value, top 10 risks Interactive meeting + push report Monthly PM Sponsor and champion Dashboard, issues needing decisions Interactive briefing Bi-weekly PM Project team Weekly update Interactive stand-up Weekly Pillar leads Fraser Health and BCCDC Data-sharing status, test results Push email + interactive working group Bi-weekly SENSE / SIMULATE leads Farmers and workers Sampling schedules, grant info (English, Punjabi and Spanish) Push text messages + interactive town halls Monthly Community liaisons Public and media Progress, dashboard, FAQs Pull website + push news releases Ongoing / at milestones Communications lead Escalation path Pillar lead · 5 working days PM · 5 more days Champion Escalation: an issue that a pillar lead cannot solve in 5 working days goes to the PM. If the PM cannot solve it in 5 more days, it goes to the champion. Status reports describe where we stand on a given date, while progress reports describe what we accomplished, and the project uses both (Renso, 2026)."},{"p":"Stakeholders","f":"stakeholders.html","id":"reports","h":"Progress reports","t":"Topic 12 Progress reports Every pillar lead sends a weekly update in the format taught in class: work completed, working on, going well, not going well, suggestions and issues, and project updates (Renso, 2026). Example: the SHIELD weekly update. SHIELD weekly update Week of July 17, 2028 · From: SHIELD pillar lead · To: PM HVAC installed in 21 of 52 wave-1 schools; air-flow tests passed in 19 Schools 22–35; re-testing 2 schools that failed Abbotsford crews are 4 days ahead of schedule Far-UVC fixture delivery delayed 2 weeks by the vendor Re-sequence UVC installs to the end of wave 1; apply the contract's late-delivery clause CPI 0.98, SV −$1.2M; risk R7 (contractor shortage) is decreasing Without regular updates, problems stay hidden until they become crises (Renso, 2026). Monthly reports also feed the earned-value numbers on the Cost page ."},{"p":"Stakeholders","f":"stakeholders.html","id":"","h":"Stakeholders","t":"A stakeholder is everyone who is affected by the project (Renso, 2026). In a pandemic project, that is almost everyone in the valley. The table lists the main stakeholders.","top":1},{"p":"Time","f":"time.html","id":"the-gantt-chart","h":"The Gantt chart","t":"The Gantt chart Hover a bar to see its exact dates. Use the buttons to show only the critical path or one pillar. All work Critical path SENSE SIMULATE SHIELD SUPPLY SUSTAIN Initiating, planning, closing Procurement SENSE SIMULATE SHIELD SUPPLY SUSTAIN Testing Milestone / amber outline = critical path Gantt chart of 34 activities and milestones. Hover a bar to see its exact dates. Use the buttons to show only the critical path or one pillar. For how the pillars work together during an outbreak, see the response flowchart on the Day 0 page ."},{"p":"Time","f":"time.html","id":"how-the-schedule-was-built","h":"How the schedule was built","t":"How the schedule was built Scheduling followed the seven steps from the lectures: plan schedule management, define activities, sequence them, estimate resources, estimate durations, develop the schedule and control it (Renso, 2026). Plan schedule management Define activities Sequence them Estimate resources Estimate durations Develop the schedule Control it Dependencies Mandatory (hard logic) The depot must be built (7.2) before the stockpile can move in (7.3). Integrated testing (9.1) cannot start until the systems exist. Discretionary I chose to finish privacy impact assessments (2.2) before any data work on the digital twin begins, even though design could technically overlap. External School HVAC work can only happen in July and August, when students are away. So SHIELD work is split into two summer waves (6.2a and 6.2b)."},{"p":"Time","f":"time.html","id":"the-critical-path","h":"The critical path","t":"The critical path The longest chain of dependent work. Any delay on this chain delays the whole project. Charter Requirements Detailed design Baseline approval Procurement Depot land Depot construction Stockpile Integrated testing Operation Sentinel Go-live Closing The longest chain of dependent work is: charter, then requirements, then detailed design, then baseline approval, then procurement, then depot land, depot construction, stockpile, integrated testing, Operation Sentinel, go-live and closing. Any delay on this chain delays the whole project. The PMO tracks it weekly, and the depot contract has late-delivery penalties. Click \"Critical path\" above to see it."},{"p":"Time","f":"time.html","id":"smart-milestones","h":"SMART milestones","t":"SMART milestones Milestones on a Gantt chart should be specific, measurable, assignable, realistic and time-framed (Renso, 2026): M1 Charter approved Feb 26, 2027 · Owner: Champion Measured by: four signatures on the charter M2 Baseline plan approved (kill point) Sep 3, 2027 · Owner: PM Measured by: steering committee vote: continue, redirect or stop M3 Major contracts awarded Nov 30, 2027 · Owner: Procurement lead Measured by: signed contracts for depot, lab, HVAC, compute M4 Go-live Oct 1, 2029 · Owner: Sponsor Measured by: Operation Sentinel passed; alert issued within 72 hours M5 Project closed Dec 31, 2029 · Owner: PM Measured by: all deliverables accepted, contracts closed, lessons learned filed"},{"p":"Time","f":"time.html","id":"pert-estimate-and-project-buffer","h":"PERT estimate and project buffer","t":"Estimating under uncertainty PERT estimate and project buffer Estimates are uncertain, so a project buffer should always exist (Renso, 2026). For the riskiest task, depot construction, I used PERT: optimistic 10 months, most likely 13 months, pessimistic 19 months. The PERT estimate is (10 + 4×13 + 19) ÷ 6 = 13.5 months, which is the duration planned on the chart. Go-live is three months before the hard close date, and that gap is the project buffer. Depot construction · PERT Optimistic 10 Most likely 13 Pessimistic 19 (10 + 4×13 + 19) ÷ 6 = 13.5 months"},{"p":"Time","f":"time.html","id":"","h":"Time: schedule and Gantt chart","t":"The work runs from January 4, 2027 to a formal close on December 31, 2029. The system goes live on October 1, 2029, which leaves three months for closing.","top":1},{"p":"Why the valley","f":"why.html","id":"what-the-2020-shutdown-cost","h":"What the 2020 shutdown cost","t":"The bill for 2020 What the 2020 shutdown cost On March 17, 2020, B.C. suspended K-12 classes indefinitely, and most of the province's 550,000 students were sent home (CBC News, 2020a). Between February and June 2020, B.C. lost 235,100 jobs (RBC Economics, 2020), and the province forecast a record $12.5 billion deficit (CBC News, 2020c). Federally, the Canada Emergency Response Benefit alone was projected to cost $71.3 billion (CBC News, 2020b). B.C.'s real GDP fell 3.1% in 2020 (BC Stats, 2024). 550,000 students sent home when classes were suspended (CBC News, 2020a) $71.3B projected cost of the Canada Emergency Response Benefit alone (CBC News, 2020b) 1.4% To put the $1 billion in perspective: it is about 1.4% of what CERB alone was projected to cost (CBC News, 2020b). If Sentinel Valley helps the region avoid even part of one future shutdown, the money has done its job. (See the ROI analysis on the Cost page .)"},{"p":"Why the valley","f":"why.html","id":"why-this-valley-is-different","h":"Why this valley is different","t":"Why this valley is different Three reasons the Fraser Valley is more exposed than most of Canada. Spillover It is where animal viruses meet people The Fraser Valley holds about 80% of B.C.'s poultry farms (CTV News, 2022). Since 2022, avian flu has forced repeated culls (CTV News, 2025). Spillover to people has already happened here. Fraser Valley poultry workers caught H7N3 in 2004 (CBC News, 2024), and in 2024 a B.C. teenager became Canada's first domestic H5N1 case, with a virus matching local wild birds (B.C. Ministry of Health, 2024b). WHO now asks countries to prepare for whole families of pathogens (WHO, 2024a). That is what a One Health design does, because it monitors animals and people together. Workers COVID hit the people who feed us During COVID-19, Fraser Health focused on outbreaks in food processing, on farms and in nurseries \"where we have workers in crowded accommodations, including our temporary farm workers\" (Fraser Health, 2021). UFV researchers have already studied this problem with federal research funding (Jardine, 2021). Sentinel Valley builds on that local knowledge. Isolation The valley can be cut off In November 2021, floods and mudslides blocked every major highway connecting the Lower Mainland to the rest of Canada, and grocery shelves emptied (CBC News, 2021). The Fraser Valley East and Abbotsford regions produce over a third of B.C.'s farm revenue (Statistics Canada, 2021). A pandemic stockpile stored in a flood zone, or far away behind closed highways, would fail when we need it. Our supply depot will be local and on high ground."},{"p":"Why the valley","f":"why.html","id":"what-the-evidence-says-works","h":"What the evidence says works","t":"What the evidence says works Each pillar is built on something already proven. SENSE Wastewater surveillance Wastewater surveillance gives early, population-level signals and is cost-effective compared with mass swab testing (Sanjak et al., 2024). B.C. already tests wastewater for several respiratory viruses (BC Centre for Disease Control [BCCDC], n.d.), so we will expand that system. SHIELD Clean indoor air Clean indoor air reduces exposure. The CDC recommends aiming for at least five air changes per hour (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention [CDC], n.d.). Far-UVC light reduced infectious airborne virus by more than 98% in an occupied room (Buonanno et al., 2024). SIMULATE Digital twins Digital twins of cities can simulate how a virus spreads and test interventions before officials use them (Bilal et al., 2025). Sentinel Valley will not stop every infection or predict an unknown virus with certainty, and only provincial and medical health officers can issue health orders. It gives those officials earlier data, cleaner buildings, supplies and trained people."},{"p":"Why the valley","f":"why.html","id":"","h":"Why the Fraser Valley needs this before the next pandemic","t":"The professor's question was how to avoid another catastrophic shutdown.","top":1}];
